meta {
  name: List Lead Sources
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/lead-sources
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

script:post-response {
  // Every request that creates or re-attributes a lead sends a `leadSourceId`,
  // and ids differ per database — so they are captured here rather than pasted
  // into request bodies. This folder sorts before `Leads`, so a full run has
  // them in hand; running a Leads request on its own needs this one run first.
  if (res.getStatus() === 200) {
    const sources = res.getBody().leadSources;
    const bySlug = (slug) => (sources.find((s) => s.slug === slug) || {}).id;
    bru.setVar("mailerLeadSourceId", bySlug("mailer"));
    bru.setVar("facebookLeadSourceId", bySlug("facebook"));
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns a leadSources array", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("leadSources");
    expect(body.leadSources).to.be.an("array");
    expect(body.leadSources.length).to.be.above(0);
  });

  test("every source carries id, name and slug — and nothing internal", function () {
    for (const source of res.getBody().leadSources) {
      expect(source).to.have.all.keys("id", "name", "slug");
    }
  });

  test("Mailer is present and sorts first", function () {
    const sources = res.getBody().leadSources;
    expect(sources[0].slug).to.equal("mailer");
  });

  test("Other, the catch-all, sorts last", function () {
    const sources = res.getBody().leadSources;
    expect(sources[sources.length - 1].slug).to.equal("other");
  });

  test("captured the ids later requests send", function () {
    expect(bru.getVar("mailerLeadSourceId")).to.match(/^[a-f0-9]{24}$/);
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /lead-sources — where a lead came from (PAC-135)

  The list behind the lead form's required **source select**, the Leads page's
  **lead-source filter**, and the Owner dashboard's lead-source filter. Returns
  platform sources unioned with the calling agency's own rows, **active only**.

  - **Module:** `leads` **or** `owner_dashboard` (either enabled for the agency)
  - **Permission:** `leads:read` **or** `owner_dashboard:read`
  - **Data scope:** none — reference data, identical for everyone in the agency

  > **Why no `lead_sources` module?** Same argument as `GET /carriers`: this is a
  > catalog read behind pages that already have gates, not a page. A module key
  > of its own would add a row to the permission matrix and hand every agency a
  > switch that silently empties the lead form's dropdown. It accepts *either*
  > gate because an account can hold the Owner dashboard without the Leads
  > module (the Data Team template does).

  ## Why a collection
  Lead source used to be a `{ code, label }` object copied onto every lead and
  deal, picked from a list hard-coded in `@sfa/shared`. Production had already
  outgrown that list — real leads carry Web, Walk-In, Other and Live Call
  Transfer — and the same source sat under several SmartSuite choice codes,
  because those codes are per-field. Leads and deals now hold a `leadSourceId`.

  ## Two kinds of row
  | `agencyId` | meaning | who writes it |
  |---|---|---|
  | `null` | platform source, visible to every agency | the core seed (`seedLeadSources`) and the `lead_sources_backfill` migration |
  | an id | that agency's own source | the migration and the SmartSuite import, from the labels that agency actually uses |

  An agency row **shadows** a platform row with the same slug, so an agency can
  reword a platform source rather than having to invent a different name.

  ## Order
  Platform sources in seeded order, then the agency's own alphabetically, then
  **Other** last. Resolved in application code: Mongo sorts a missing
  `displayOrder` *before* every number, which would put an agency's vendors above
  Mailer.

  ## Archived sources
  `active: false` takes a source out of this list — and out of what
  `POST /leads` / `PATCH /leads/:id` will accept — but the row stays, so a lead or
  sale attributed to it years ago still renders its name.

  ## Curation is deliberately not here
  Seeded only. Super-admin CRUD over the platform rows and agency-settings CRUD
  over an agency's own are future work; the schema already carries `agencyId`,
  `active` and `displayOrder` so both drop in as controllers.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  {
    "leadSources": [
      { "id": "66f0…a1", "name": "Mailer", "slug": "mailer" },
      { "id": "66f0…a2", "name": "Book of Business", "slug": "book-of-business" },
      { "id": "66f0…b7", "name": "Waterstone", "slug": "waterstone" },
      { "id": "66f0…a8", "name": "Other", "slug": "other" }
    ]
  }
  ```

  | field | notes |
  |---|---|
  | id   | what `leadSourceId` takes on `POST /leads`, `PATCH /leads/:id` and `GET /leads` |
  | name | what is rendered |
  | slug | stable key — a rename does not change it |

  ## Errors
  | status | when |
  |---|---|
  | 401 | no / expired token |
  | 403 | neither `leads:read` nor `owner_dashboard:read`, or neither module enabled |
}
